        <h2 id='why-scams-happen'>Why fake color prediction platforms are everywhere</h2>
        <p>Color prediction games became popular because they are simple to understand and easy to promote. That popularity also attracts scammers who clone designs, copy names, and run short-lived websites. Many fake platforms rely on urgency—limited-time bonuses, “VIP earning”, or “instant withdrawal”—to push users to deposit quickly without reading rules.</p>
        <p>The good news is that most fake platforms leave patterns. If you use a structured checklist, you can filter out risky sites fast. This guide focuses on practical checks that normal users can do in minutes, without technical tools.</p>
        <h2 id='domain-check'>1) Domain and link verification (the fastest filter)</h2>
        <p>Start with the URL. Legit platforms keep one primary domain and consistent branding across pages. Fake sites often use look-alike spellings, extra hyphens, or different TLDs. Compare the domain carefully letter-by-letter. If you reached the site from a random comment, open a fresh tab and type the domain yourself or use a trusted page with official buttons.</p>
        <p>Also pay attention to redirects. A clean site usually loads directly and stays on the same domain. If the page jumps through multiple short links, or it forces a file download, treat it as high risk. For safety, our Login and Sign Up buttons use one official invitation link.</p>
        <h2 id='policy-pages'>2) Real rules pages: terms, privacy, and game rules</h2>
        <p>Even basic legit platforms try to show terms, privacy policy, and game rules. You don’t need to read every word, but scan for concrete details: minimum deposit/withdrawal, processing time, KYC requirements, and bonus conditions. Fake platforms either have no policy pages, use broken links, or paste generic text that doesn’t match the app.</p>
        <p>Another quick clue: check if the rules mention the same platform name consistently. If the site calls itself ‘TC Game’ in one section and a totally different brand in another, it may be copied content.</p>
        <h2 id='support-signals'>3) Support signals that are actually useful</h2>
        <p>A real support system is not just a Telegram number. Look for an in-app ticket system, a clear contact email, or a help center with FAQs. Scammers often use one chat handle and push you to share sensitive information. Never share OTPs, passwords, or banking PINs with anyone.</p>
        <p>If a platform support agent asks you to deposit more to “unlock withdrawal”, that’s a major red flag. Legit rules are written upfront; they don’t change in DMs.</p>
        <h2 id='wallet-risk'>4) Deposits, bonuses, and withdrawal red flags</h2>
        <p>Most user complaints start in the wallet section. Before you deposit, check whether the site supports common payment methods with clear instructions. Suspicious signs include: forcing crypto only, requesting direct transfer to personal accounts, or showing constantly changing QR codes without verification.</p>
        <p>For withdrawals, look for a stated processing window and minimum withdrawal amount. If the platform advertises ‘instant withdraw’ but never explains timing or verification, be careful. Also read bonus rules: some bonuses require turnover before withdrawal. If this is unclear, skip the bonus and play only what you can afford.</p>
        <h2 id='ui-consistency'>5) UI consistency and “too-perfect” claims</h2>
        <p>Cloned apps often look like a screenshot collage: some buttons don’t work, some pages are missing, or the language changes randomly. Legit sites can still be imperfect, but key actions (register, deposit, withdraw, help) should work consistently.</p>
        <p>Be skeptical of claims like “sure shot trick”, “100% win”, or “guaranteed color prediction”. In reality, prediction games are risky. Honest guides talk about responsible play, not guaranteed profits.</p>
        <h2 id='safe-start'>A safer way to start if you still want to explore</h2>
        <p>If you choose to explore a platform, start with non-sensitive actions: browse rules, check FAQs, and confirm the official domain. When you register, use a strong password and keep your login private. Avoid installing unknown APKs from untrusted sources; prefer official stores or well-known distribution methods.</p>
        <p>Finally, treat any money you add as entertainment budget. Do not borrow, do not chase losses, and step back if the experience feels stressful. A good platform is transparent; a bad one pressures you.</p>
